<% sweep = Lab::SWEEPS.fetch("logic") %>
<% seeds = sweep.fetch(:seeds) %>
<% epochs = number_with_delimiter sweep.fetch(:epochs), delimiter: " " %>
<% bundle = Lab::LogicReading::FULL_BUNDLE %>
<% constants = Lab::LogicReading %>
<% record_url = "https://github.com/hanscanonico/life_simulator/blob/main/docs/design_record.md" %>

<h2>Question</h2>
<p>
  <%= link_to "Paid to compute", finding_path("paid-computation-stops-at-one-step-tasks") %>,
  BFF replicators took the arithmetic tasks one substitution of a copier computes and went no
  further. The design study's diagnosis was the instruction set: BFF has no two-input data op,
  so every deeper task needed a counted loop, and no partial loop earns anything on the way.
  Logic, the variant of the Metabolism substrate in DESIGN §1.4, keeps that economy and that
  pay and adds the missing part: one NAND byte, run only by the assay, with Avida's logic
  tasks as the ladder. Every rung is a composition of NANDs and a lower rung is a part of a
  higher one. <strong>Given the parts, and paid for each, does a soup assemble the deep rungs,
  XOR and EQU?</strong>
</p>

<h2>Method</h2>
<p>
  The parents are the emerged worlds the
  <%= link_to "from-emerged sweep", experiment_path("from-emerged") %> continued, chosen by its
  rule, the same parents and seeds as the Metabolism sweep. Each terminal world runs another
  <%= epochs %> epochs, seeds <%= seeds.first %>–<%= seeds.last %>, in three arms. All three run
  the Metabolism economy (influx <%= number_with_delimiter bundle.fetch("energy_influx"), delimiter: " " %>,
  a stock cap of <%= number_with_delimiter bundle.fetch("energy_stock_cap"), delimiter: " " %>, no theft)
  and the logic assay every <%= bundle.fetch("task_every") %> epochs. In the
  <strong>full</strong> arm every rung pays, at
  <%= number_with_delimiter bundle.fetch("task_reward"), delimiter: " " %> energy a unit. In the
  <strong>deep-only</strong> arm only XOR and EQU pay, Lenski's control: it separates "the deep
  rungs are built on paid parts" from "the deep rungs are directly reachable". In the
  <strong>none</strong> arm nothing pays. ECHO, NOT, NAND, AND, ORN, OR, ANDN and NOR are the
  rungs a NAND circuit can compute reading each input once. XOR and EQU, the deep rungs, are
  not: every NAND circuit for either reads an input or an intermediate twice.
</p>
<p>
  The reading is <strong>pre-registered</strong>, with every number fixed before a child was
  seeded: <%= link_to "docs/design_record.md", record_url %>, 2026-10-01, "Logic: does a soup
  assemble features beyond its one-step rungs when the parts are paid?", DESIGN §1.3 item 16.
  Each full child is paired with the child of the same parent and seed in a control arm. All
  are read past a settling window of
  <%= number_with_delimiter constants::SETTLING_WINDOW, delimiter: " " %> epochs, and a pair
  with an extinct child is in no test. There are four one-sided sign tests at
  p &lt; <%= constants::SIGN_TEST_LEVEL %>:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>H-capability-L</strong> (full against none): the last-decile median
    <span class="mono">logic_capability</span>, the number of rungs held by a tenth of the
    world.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>H-deep</strong>, the rung-4 question (full against none): the same count over XOR
    and EQU, <span class="mono">logic_capability_deep</span>.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>H-stones</strong> (full against deep-only): H-deep's count, against the arm paid
    for the deep rungs alone.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>H-complexity</strong> (full against none): the from-emerged rule, under which the
    dominant replicator's executed instruction count rises when its last-decile median is at
    least 1.2 times its first. It reads only pairs in which neither child relapsed.
  </li>
</ul>
<p>
  Each test is also read with the extinct pairs kept and without the four parents the design
  study piloted, and a shown test lists any one or two parents whose pairs it cannot do
  without.
</p>

<% reading = @show.logic %>
<h2>Result</h2>
<% if reading&.any? %>
  <p>
    <% reading.tests.each do |test| %>
      <span class="badge <%= test.badge_class %>"><%= test.hypothesis %> <%= test.outcome_label %></span>
    <% end %>
    <% if reading.deep_climbed? %>
      <strong>Paid parts built a deep rung: see the readings below.</strong>
    <% else %>
      <strong>Given a NAND, paid replicators climbed the tasks that read their inputs once and
      none that needs one twice.</strong>
    <% end %>
    <% if reading.interim? %>
      Not every child of the sweep has finished, so this reading is interim.
    <% end %>
  </p>
  <ul id="logic-finding-tests">
    <% reading.tests.each do |test| %>
      <% comparison = test.comparison %>
      <% leaning = reading.leaning(test) %>
      <% treatment = test.treatment.name %>
      <% control = test.control.name %>
      <li class="mb-3">
        <strong><%= test.hypothesis %></strong>, <%= treatment %> against <%= control %>:
        <%= comparison.favouring %> pairs favour <%= treatment %>, <%= comparison.against %>
        <%= control %> and <%= comparison.ties %> tie, of <%= comparison.measured_count %>
        measured<% if comparison.p_value %>, p = <%= p_value_text(comparison.p_value) %><% end %>,
        <span class="badge <%= test.badge_class %>"><%= test.outcome_label %></span>.
        <% if reading.unanimous?(test) %>
          Every pair of all <%= reading.parent_count %> parents favours <%= treatment %>.
        <% else %>
          By parent, <%= leaning.full %> lean to <%= treatment %>, <%= leaning.tied %> tie
          throughout and <%= leaning.control %> lean to <%= control %>.
        <% end %>
        <% [["With the extinct pairs kept", reading.kept(test.hypothesis)],
            ["Without the piloted parents", reading.unpiloted(test.hypothesis)]].each do |label, sensitivity| %>
          <%= label %>: <%= sensitivity.comparison.favouring %> to
          <%= sensitivity.comparison.against %> with <%= sensitivity.comparison.ties %> ties<% if sensitivity.comparison.p_value %>,
          p = <%= p_value_text(sensitivity.comparison.p_value) %><% end %>,
          <span class="badge <%= sensitivity.badge_class %>"><%= sensitivity.outcome_label %></span>.
        <% end %>
      </li>
    <% end %>
  </ul>
  <% if reading.shown_tests.any? %>
    <p id="logic-finding-leave-out">
      <% if reading.carried_tests.empty? %>
        No one or two parents carry a shown test: whichever one or two are left out, the rest
        still read p &lt; <%= constants::SIGN_TEST_LEVEL %>.
      <% else %>
        <% reading.carried_tests.each do |test| %>
          <%= test.hypothesis %> rests on parents
          <%= test.comparison.carried_by.map { |parents| parents.map { |id| "run #{id}" }.join(" and ") }.to_sentence(two_words_connector: " or ", last_word_connector: ", or ") %>.
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    </p>
  <% end %>
  <% full = reading.full_arm %>
  <p id="logic-finding-losses">
    The full arm lost <%= pluralize(reading.extinct_children(full), "child") %> to extinction,
    and <%= reading.relapsed_children(full) %> relapsed past the settling window; the deep-only
    arm lost <%= reading.extinct_children(reading.deep_only_arm) %> and
    <%= reading.relapsed_children(reading.deep_only_arm) %>, the none arm
    <%= reading.extinct_children(reading.none_arm) %> and
    <%= reading.relapsed_children(reading.none_arm) %>.
  </p>
  <p class="text-sm text-muted">
    Every count on this page is read from the sweep's stored samples at render time, through
    the same reading <%= link_to "the sweep page", experiment_path("logic", anchor: "logic-reading") %>
    draws.
  </p>

  <h2>The ladder</h2>
  <% income = reading.income_share %>
  <p id="logic-finding-ladder">
    Descriptively, counting a rung where it held a tenth of a child's world for
    <%= constants::PERSISTENCE_RUN %> samples running: of <%= full.children.size %> full
    children, the worlds reached
    <%= reading.read_once_tasks.map { |task| "#{task.upcase} in #{reading.reached(full, task)}" }.to_sentence %>;
    <%= constants::DEEP_TASKS.map { |task| "#{task.upcase} in #{reading.reached(full, task)}" }.to_sentence %>.
    <% [reading.deep_only_arm, reading.none_arm].each do |arm| %>
      <% held = constants::TASKS.select { |task| reading.reached(arm, task).positive? } %>
      Of <%= arm.children.size %> <%= arm.name %> children,
      <% if held.empty? %>
        none reached a rung.
      <% else %>
        the worlds reached <%= held.map { |task| "#{task.upcase} in #{reading.reached(arm, task)}" }.to_sentence %>
        and no other rung.
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
    <%= reading.deep_children(full) %> full, <%= reading.deep_children(reading.deep_only_arm) %>
    deep-only and <%= reading.deep_children(reading.none_arm) %> none children reached a deep
    rung, so <%= reading.arms.sum { |arm| reading.stepping_stones(arm) } %> climbed one through
    OR, ANDN or NOR, the stepping stones of Lenski et al. 2003.
    <% if income %>
      Tasks gave a full cell about <%= percent_value income.mean %> of its income, between
      <%= percent_value income.low %> and <%= percent_value income.high %> across the children,
      an estimate that ignores the stock cap.
    <% end %>
  </p>

  <%= render "experiments/logic_reading", reading: reading.report %>
<% else %>
  <p class="text-muted">
    No child of <%= link_to "the sweep", experiment_path("logic") %> is in this database, so
    there is nothing to read here. This page reads the children at render time and states the
    claim from them.
  </p>
<% end %>

<h2>What a refuted H-deep means</h2>
<p>
  On the lab, read-only queries over every stored sample of the 162 children found
  <span class="mono">logic_capability_deep</span> at 0 in every one. The assay never credited
  XOR to more than 5 of the 256 cells it drew, in one sample of run 4817, nor EQU to more than
  1, against the 26 a tenth of the world needs. With no deep rung in any arm the tests cannot
  favour the full ladder, and the pre-registration reads a tie throughout as refuted. H-deep
  needed only 5 discordant pairs, and 0 of 54 full children puts a two-sided 95% upper bound
  of about 0.066 on the rate at which a paid child reaches a deep rung in this budget. Child
  for child, the deep-only worlds read exactly as the unpaid ones in every column of the
  report: with no XOR or EQU ever credited, their pay was never collected, and H-stones ties
  as H-deep does.
</p>
<p>
  So given a NAND and paid for every part, BFF copiers climb every rung that reads its inputs
  once and stop below the two that need an input twice. The pre-registration read this
  outcome as closing the "ladder of compositions" route, with mutational supply and a code
  region apart from the copied tape as the next suspects. A design study written before this
  reading, and summarised in the Meta-stack pre-registration in the
  <%= link_to "design record", record_url %> (2026-10-02, "Meta-stack: does a soup assemble the
  deep rungs from paid parts on a metabolism tape read with a stack NAND?"), separated them.
  Its numbers are pilot numbers from a
  scratch copy of the engine, not findings. Supply did not bind: a specific substitution
  arrived somewhere in a world every 128 epochs, the read-once rungs came on schedule, and
  mutation rates of 4 and 8 times the default made no deep rung, while 16 times melted the
  copiers. Nor did the copier alone: a code region the soup never runs climbed to NOR and
  stopped there. What binds, on the study's reading, is <strong>fan-out in a two-head machine
  whose NAND writes in place</strong>. A deep rung needs a value twice, and a NAND that
  overwrites its operand leaves nothing to read again without a stored copy and re-aligned
  heads. Its follow-up tried a NAND that keeps its operands, and in the pilot that code region,
  at a raised mutation rate, then held XOR or EQU at a tenth of the world in 2 of 3 seeds.
</p>
<p>
  H-complexity not being shown sets this sweep apart from Metabolism, where paid complexity
  rose: here pay did not make the dominant replicator execute more of its tape, 2 pairs
  against 1.
</p>

<h2>What comes next</h2>
<p>
  <%= link_to "Meta-stack", experiment_path(Lab.slug_for("meta_stack")) %>, DESIGN §1.3 item 17,
  seeded right after this reading, asks this sweep's question on the machine the study points
  at: a second, 32-byte tape the soup never executes, read by the logic assay with the NAND
  that keeps its operands, against an arm paid for the deep rungs alone and an arm with this
  sweep's in-place NAND. It runs on the same parents and pairs its children with this sweep's.
</p>

<h2>What is not claimed</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Nothing about rung 4 on Soup.</strong> This substrate imports an objective and a
    primitive: whatever rose here was paid for by tasks the programme chose, computed with a
    byte the programme added. It is never pooled with the fitness-free runs, and rung 4 on
    Soup stays "not shown".
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Not "XOR is unreachable".</strong> It is one machine and one budget, and what the
    NAND byte writes was the programme's choice. The study's account of why it stops is pilot,
    and Meta-stack is what tests it.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>A finite ladder.</strong> EQU is its top, so complexity here is at most five NANDs
    deep, and the inputs are the assay's case sets.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Children are correlated.</strong> Three children share each parent's world, four
    of the parents were piloted, and the none arm repeats the Metabolism sweep's unpaid worlds.
    That is why every test is read by parent, with the extinct pairs kept and without the
    piloted parents.
  </li>
</ul>
